From 43fe5ab8d9347d27a26e3aeddfa396d69242a923 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 01:42:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/5] test: 157 cases against the phase 6 surface, most of them
 trying to break it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

631 -> 788. All 631 earlier cases stay green without edits, and one of them —
the permission matrix parity check — is what caught the capability that had been
missing from `can()` since FASE 0.

Two files earn their place:

`phase6-project-boundaries` exists because `security definer` hides
authorization from the Postgres catalog. There is no policy for a sweep to
check, so an `if` deleted in a refactor would break nothing. Every refusal
written in those function bodies has a case here — including the one that
matters most: a project whose second write fails leaves NO project behind, and a
sweep proves no project anywhere has zero stages.

`phase6-no-demo-project` reads the source instead of a transcribed list, for the
same reason `phase5-messages` does: a list would age on the day someone
reintroduced `DEMO_PROJECT_ID`, which is the day the test needs to fail. It also
guards the mock boundary and the absence of percentages in the journey.

The rest: isolation in pairs (what each role sees AND what it must not),
immutability of `client_id`/`journey_key`/`type`, the partial-index invariant,
projections checked against the production column constants, the pure journey
derivation, and the portal resolution across zero/one/several, draft, paused,
completed and archived.

